@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        Justification is the article by which the church stands or falls
    </span>
    <span>Martin Luther</span>
</div>
    
<ol>
    <li>
        God is the most perfect and righteous Judge, exonerating the innocent and bringing recompense to the wicked.
    </li>
    <li>
        Like the judges of old Israel, God not only pronounces His judgements but He executes them as well.
        God justifies the righteous by delivering them from their enemies, and he judges the wicked 
        by bringing calamity upon them.
    </li>
    <li>
        Through the prophets, God promised to declare Israel as His people once more, and to free them from
        the ultimate enemy of sin and death. God would justify Israel for His name's sake in spite of their sins,
        and He would justify them in order to cleanse them from their sins.
    </li>
    <li>
        All men have sinned and fallen short of the glory of God, and none can merit justification
        through his own works. But God, being rich in mercy, seeks to justify sinners by faith alone for His
        name's sake.
    </li>
    <li>
        To fulfill His promise, God sent Jesus Christ to atone for the sins of the world and to be vindicated as 
        the Son of God in the resurrection. Christ is the Justified One, the one who God has exalted as righteous,
        and the one through whom all others become righteous.
    </li>
    <li>
        When a person is united to Christ by faith they are also united to Christ's justification: their sin is
        imputed to Christ, and Christ's righteousness is counted as theirs.
    </li>
    <li>
        Just as God vindicated Christ in the resurrection, those who are justified in Christ are also raised to newness
        of life. For God's judgements are not in vain, and He fulfills his judgement of 
        righteousness by making sinners righteous through the Spirit.
    </li>
    <li>
        On the last day of judgement God will vindicate His work one last time: those who have done works of the
        flesh will die, and those who have done works of the Spirit will live. No works will
        obligate God to give a righteous verdict, but they will reveal God's judgement: 
        those who are justified will be declared righteous before God and before the world,
        being forgiven of all their sins and the imperfections in their works, and finally
        they will be glorified with the saints in the new heavens and the new earth.
    </li>
